## P0 — request lifecycle and execution isolation regressions (2026-11-19)

- [ ] **Gemini stream interruptions and duplicate full-request retries.**
  - Reproduce the repeated `Gemini stream interrupted; restarting the full request (attempt N/4)` warnings.
  - Preserve and report the underlying error currently discarded by the retry callback; classify transport failures, HTTP failures, malformed/incomplete SSE events, missing finishReason, and empty responses separately.
  - Trace actual provider/client dispatch for each report; NVIDIA/OpenAI-compatible requests must never enter Gemini retry handling.
  - Do not restart the full generation after any response text has already been emitted unless the provider supports a verified resume cursor. Preserve partial output and return a clear interruption error instead of duplicating the answer.
  - Test cancellation, retry-before-first-byte, EOF after partial text, validation failures, and provider-specific dispatch.

- [ ] **Ctrl+C interruption must terminate the active operation promptly and deterministically.**
  - Reproduce `Interrupting; waiting for the active operation to stop.` followed by delayed `Interrupted`.
  - Trace cancellation from the TUI request context through HTTP streaming, retry backoff, tool execution, shell subprocesses, and worker operations.
  - Identify waits that do not observe context cancellation; ensure every cancellable stage exits and the TUI returns to an input-ready state.
  - Do not start a replacement request while the old request/tool process is still running; surface a specific timeout or non-cancellable-operation diagnostic when shutdown cannot complete promptly.
  - Test interruption during model streaming, retry backoff, tool dispatch, shell execution, and provider response parsing.

- [ ] **P0: `/clear` must isolate the next prompt from all previous execution.**
  - Reproduce the report: after `/clear`, a new prompt explicitly says not to use tools, but AGY nevertheless calls `list_files`, reads `ai.log` and `README.md`, then answers.
  - Determine whether those calls are delayed tool calls from an earlier in-flight request, or fresh calls prompted by mandatory project instructions/tool declarations injected into the new request. Do not assume either cause without tracing request IDs and tool-call origins.
  - `NewSession` currently replaces session history and resets active context; verify whether it also cancels/invalidate any prior active generation, pending tool-call batch, authorization response, queued event, and background task before accepting another prompt.
  - Confirmed `composeSystemInstruction()` loads available `AGENTS.md`, `Agents.rst`, and `.github/copilot-instructions.md` from the workspace and injects them into every request. This repository's `Agents.rst` requires reading `ai.log` at the start of every task, which can explain instruction-driven file reads even after `/clear`. Verify this against request IDs/tool-call origins; the additional `README.md` read is not yet explained.
  - Scope repository workflow requirements to repository-maintenance tasks. A plain writing request that explicitly forbids tools must not trigger automatic repository reads merely because project instructions are present.
  - Give each logical run a generation/session epoch. Reject late model responses and tool calls from an older epoch; cancellation must be checked immediately before every tool execution and before every follow-up model request.
  - Ensure a cleared session cannot execute a tool solely because an earlier request returned a tool call after the clear boundary.
  - Audit mandatory `Agents.rst`/workspace instructions: project startup reads must not be injected as unconditional per-prompt tool actions that defeat an explicit no-tools request. Keep safety requirements, but separate repository-agent workflow rules from ordinary user prompts.
  - Tests: clear during model streaming; clear after a tool-call response but before execution; clear while a tool is running; late completion from prior run; fresh no-tools prompt after clear; ensure no old-run tool output appears in the new session.

